| **2** | **A completion-certificate layer** supporting prime-support density, independently proved Hilbert bounds, and structural presentations | Can close a fiber without repeating a large saturation. It must distinguish support equality from graded-ring equality. The consultation already identifies density in an irreducible support as an alternative. [`consult:141`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:141) | A certificate that merely reproduces \(K_2\)’s own invariants, or checks several unchanged degrees, supplies no independent lower bound. |
| **3** | **A second-prime and Gaussian-rational reconstruction harness** | Detects exceptional-prime behavior and turns modular candidates into characteristic-zero identities after verification. The producer already distinguishes \(\mathbb Q(i)\) from the two split embeddings. [`producer:119–123`](~/worktrees/IBMQuantum/a5-kernel-attachments/docs/evidence/a5-kernel-attachments/nullity16_rees_fibers.py:119), [`634–650`](~/worktrees/IBMQuantum/a5-kernel-attachments/docs/evidence/a5-kernel-attachments/nullity16_rees_fibers.py:634) | Prime-dependent generator counts, leading ideals, ranks, or failed rational membership stop promotion of the result. |
| **4** | **A matrix-export and certified-rank interface**, initially FLINT, optionally LinBox | Makes large coefficient problems measurable and interchangeable. Sparse Macaulay upper/lower bounds can certify an individual degree when standard-basis construction becomes excessive. [`consult:427–442`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:427) | Matrix construction or polynomial reduction dominates runtime, or sparse rank loses to FLINT after conversion and certificate costs. |

The degree driver should retain, for each pair, field, and degree: input hashes; original generator identities; ordered target monomials; cumulative certified relations; source standard basis; normal forms including zero slots; matrix dimensions and nonzero count; rank and relation witnesses; stage timings; and memory/termination records. Multiplication should reduce modulo \(J\) after each factor, following the successful cubic inputs. [`X cubic input:12–20`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/s1/input.sing:12)

The driver should report a finite degree as **certified**, while reporting full-fiber completeness as a separate status. Its mathematical basis is
\[
h_d=\dim_k\operatorname{span}\operatorname{NF}_{J+\mathfrak m P_d}(P_d).
\]
The quotient is annihilated by \(\mathfrak m\), which explains why global ideal membership recovers the local graded piece. [`consult:35–57`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:35)

For faster completion, I would pursue three arguments in order:

1. **Prime support plus an independent dimension witness.** For X, retain the prime defining the reduced quadratic support and construct a family of genuine limiting kernel planes with three-dimensional image. Containment and equal dimension inside that irreducible support would prove reduced-support equality over the established field. The family needs a density or function-field argument; a finite collection of arcs supplies a lower sample only. The existing X support calculation makes this route unusually concrete. [`support log:4–5`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pC_xm_reduce.log:4)

2. **A structural presentation with a proved Hilbert series.** E8-bar→VB deserves the first recognition experiment. Its reported values \(10,34,80,155\), dimension three, and degree seven are consistent with
   \[
   H(z)=\frac{1+6z}{(1-z)^4}.
   \]
   That arithmetic is a hypothesis generator, not a computed full Hilbert series. Search for a small determinantal or scroll presentation and verify ideal equality. A \(2\times7\) matrix has 21 two-by-two minors, matching the quadratic-relation count; failure to find and verify such a presentation ends that particular hypothesis. The input measurements are the recorded VB quadratic model. [`decision-record.md:157–159`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:157)

3. **An independently bounded full Hilbert function.** If a proven relation-type bound limits every minimal fiber relation to degree \(D\), method A through \(D\) closes the algebra. Alternatively, a surjection from a candidate algebra to \(F(I)\), together with an independently established equal Hilbert series, proves equality degree by degree. Regularity of the candidate \(k[y]/K_2\) supplies neither condition; that shortcut was correctly rejected. [`decision-record.md:24`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:24)

A Cohen–Macaulay or regular-sequence argument must prove something about the relevant presentation. The generator counts already exceed the germ dimensions, so the supplied ideals \(I\) cannot themselves be regular sequences. A useful alternative is an unmixed candidate algebra plus independently established equal dimension and multiplicity: a nonzero kernel would then contribute top-dimensional multiplicity.

**X illustrates why the hypotheses matter.** Its quadratic scheme and radical have the same dimension and degree, while their Hilbert functions differ: \(15,51,122,\ldots\) versus \(12,45,112,\ldots\). Thus dimension and degree alone already fail to distinguish two different graded structures in the retained example. Reduced-support completion would leave those nilpotent directions unresolved. [`rad_xm.log:1–14`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/review-checks/claude/rad_xm.log:1)

The modular harness must regenerate coefficients from the original Gaussian-rational source. Replacing `32009` in an already specialized numeric input cannot recover another reduction. It should evaluate both images of \(i\), normalize bases before matching coefficients, perform Chinese-remainder/rational reconstruction, and verify the lifted identities over \(\mathbb Q(i)\). Agreement at two primes is supporting evidence; the characteristic-zero membership and completeness argument provide the certificate. Preserve the existing retained records as predecessors.

For packages, I would use the installed stack first:

| Package or route | Concrete use and build decision | Payoff falsifier |
|---|---|---|
| **Macaulay2 ReesAlgebra** | Benchmark its symmetric-algebra presentation against the current pivot equations on VB or X. Its implementation saturates a symmetric-algebra ideal using a suitable nonzerodivisor, so it changes the presentation but can retain the saturation bottleneck. [`ReesAlgebra.m2:146–161`](/usr/share/Macaulay2/ReesAlgebra.m2:146) | Comparable or greater resource use without a completed certificate. |
| **MultigradedImplicitization** | Test whether a verified grading splits the actual quotient-map problem into substantially smaller blocks. The installed package targets homogeneous kernels of polynomial maps; applicability here needs demonstration. [`package:457–467`](/usr/share/Macaulay2/MultigradedImplicitization.m2:457) | Trivial grading, weak block reduction, or an encoding that changes \(J\) or the desired fiber. |
| **Normaliz / 4ti2** | Build only after proving a semigroup presentation or a suitable flat degeneration. Use it for the resulting cone/semigroup Hilbert problem. [`consult:467`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:467) | The presentation fails equality; normalization changes the ring; degeneration lacks a flatness certificate. |
| **CoCoA-5 / OSCAR** | One bounded independent benchmark on a reduced target, packaged under the owner’s PKGBUILDs tree if retained. Their completion advantage remains unmeasured. [`consult:468`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:468) | Another unfinished equivalent computation with no smaller intermediate problem. |
| **LinBox / FFLAS-FFPACK** | Extend or tune the installed backend after matrix measurements justify it. Retain exact modular rank witnesses. [`consult:440,466`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:440) | Sparse fill-in, conversion costs, or certificate generation erase the advantage. |
| **msolve master** | Build only for an identified relevant change and a controlled benchmark. The retained native 0.11 comparison established no speedup, and its final degree-seven line records selected/remaining pairs rather than matrix dimensions. [`compute-flags.md:168`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:168), [`native log:97`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/azure-vm2/results/e8_np_msolve_v011.log:97) | The same monomial-table growth and memory boundary recur. |
| **FastMinors** | Consider upstream work preserving Pfaffian/matrix structure. The package computes polynomial minors; the current inputs already contain expanded generators. [`FastMinors.m2:1415–1417`](/usr/share/Macaulay2/FastMinors.m2:1415), [`producer:291–294`](~/worktrees/IBMQuantum/a5-kernel-attachments/docs/evidence/a5-kernel-attachments/nullity16_basepoints.py:291) | Generator construction is a small fraction of runtime. |

I would **defer GPU rank infrastructure**. The completed logs show thousands of seconds in standard-basis construction before matrix rank is available. GPU acceleration cannot remove that polynomial bottleneck. First export a matrix whose exact rank dominates end-to-end cost; then compare a finite-field implementation against FLINT/LinBox, including transfer and certification. Floating-point rank and characteristic-two substitution would change the evidential problem. [`E8 timing:1–4`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/e8_a2/out.log:1), [`W8 timing:1–4`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/runs/w8_a2/out.log:1), [`consult:440,470`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-v2.md:440)

I would also avoid rebuilding the whole algebra stack, repeating the old unrestricted graph elimination, launching extra sign-equivalent jobs, or treating `DegreeLimit` as protection for Macaulay2 `reesIdeal`: the installed implementation explicitly says that option has no effect. [`ReesAlgebra.m2:113–122`](/usr/share/Macaulay2/ReesAlgebra.m2:113)

Placement should follow the measured mechanism and an aggregate memory budget. The host incident involved several concurrent workloads, a terminal scope using about 64 GB plus 27 GB of swap, and an uncapped Singular job. The later W8 failure occurred inside its own 6-GiB cgroup, a different boundary. The record supports controlling concurrency; it does not identify one process as the sole cause of the terminal-scope kill. [`decision-record.md:125–136`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:125)

The following are **proposed budgets**, not completion predictions. The listed substrate capacities come from the consultation resource inventory. [`codex-consult-brief.md:19–25`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/codex-consult-brief.md:19)

| Substrate | Jobs to place there | Memory and time budget |
|---|---|---|
| **Host** | Driver development; symmetry and relation checks; small target-ideal decomposition; VB completion experiments; short X/Iso structural probes | One heavy job at **8–16 GiB**, optionally one **4-GiB** small job. Keep the aggregate admitted algebra budget at **24 GiB** initially. Give exploratory certificates **15–60 minutes**; give VB A/B attempts **1–3 hours** before reviewing the result. |
| **Kaggle CPU** | Second-prime degree replays; longer X/Iso A calculations; E8/W8 cubic calculations whose measured footprint fits | **24-GiB working budget**, one heavy process, **10-hour solver deadline** within the twelve-hour session. Reserve time and memory for export and collection. Use container limits and a watchdog where delegated cgroups are unavailable. |
| **Azure VM, one per region** | Large full-fiber saturation, E8-bar→N−, and matrix constructions that exceed Kaggle’s measured envelope | One heavy job per VM. Start at **64 GiB**, increasing toward **72 GiB** only after measuring operating-system headroom. Use **6–10-hour attempts**, stage exports, and an independent VM deallocation deadline. Run the E8-bar and W8 completion jobs sequentially. |
| **GitHub Actions** | Deterministic relation verification, provenance/schema checks, symmetry checks, small replay controls; scheduled bounded independent checks | **8–12 GiB** job budget. Keep ordinary PR additions short; use scheduled/manual jobs of **30 minutes–2 hours** for larger verification. The cited runner envelope is 16 GiB and six hours, leaving little room for discovery computations near that ceiling. |

The current VM 2 case allocates `78G` per job; VM 1 records two `38G` allocations. Those are recorded choices, not safe defaults to copy: the documents alternate “84 GB” and “84 GiB.” A future launcher should read `MemTotal`, budget in bytes, and reserve explicit headroom. [`VM case:17–25`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/azure-cases/vm2-fiber-b.md:17), [`decision-record.md:143–146`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:143)

Every heavy launch should have its own memory cgroup, zero swap allowance, an active wall deadline, and recorded peak/events. The admission check must account for all simultaneously reachable allocations. A queue should release work after observing the actual predecessor unit exit. Deadline extensions should replace or reconfigure an active timer; the supervisor must remain runnable. The repository already requires memory/time/flag review and distinguishes resident-memory limits from address-space limits. [`compute-flags.md:51–65`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:51), [`179–196`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:179)

The next concrete milestone should be **one complete, independently checked fiber receipt**, preferably E8-bar→VB if its small target presentation yields to structural recognition. That receipt should connect full algebra, embedded components, characteristic-zero verification, and the remaining real attachment obligation. It would validate the machinery needed to turn the other five rows from quadratic candidates into roadmap-item-6 evidence.

